`_populate_api_route_state()`

https://github.com/fastapi/fastapi/blob/[redacted]/fastapi/routing.py#L897

extracts `stream_item_type` from the return annotation only when `response_model` is a `DefaultPlaceholder`

https://github.com/fastapi/fastapi/blob/[redacted]/fastapi/routing.py#L933

The first `__init__` correctly does this (since `response_model=Default(None)` is the default). However, inside the `__init__`, `response_model` is then set to `None`.

When `_EffectiveRouteContext.from_api_route()`

https://github.com/fastapi/fastapi/blob/[redacted]/fastapi/routing.py#L1367

later re-creates the route during `include_router`, it passes `response_model=route.response_model`

https://github.com/fastapi/fastapi/blob/[redacted]/fastapi/routing.py#L1379

 which is now `None` (not `DefaultPlaceholder`). The second call to `_populate_api_route_state` skips the extraction entirely, leaving `stream_item_type` and `stream_item_field` as `None`. The OpenAPI generator

https://github.com/fastapi/fastapi/blob/[redacted]/fastapi/openapi/utils.py#L376

then falls back to the plain SSE envelope.

The fix is in `from_api_route()` (or the `_populate_api_route_state()` call site within it): when `route.response_model` is `None` but the route is an SSE stream, `Default(None)` should be passed instead to preserve the original intent.

